// Pins the session-sibling replay carve-out (query.rs). At --sessions 2 the
// attacker can swap session 2's honestly signed pair (na#2, sg#2) into
// session 1's Bob: SIGNVERIF passes, because sk is long-term and shared, so
// both sessions' signatures verify under the same key. But Alice honestly
// sent exactly that pair to Bob in session 2, so non-injective agreement
// holds and this must stay a replay, not an authentication attack: a0 at
// both one and two sessions. Reporting a1 here would be a false attack —
// the cardinal regression. Forgery proper stays impossible (sk never
// leaks), and mixed pairs (na#2 with session 1's sg) fail SIGNVERIF and
// halt Bob.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private sk
	pk = PUBKEY(sk)
	generates na
	sg = SIGN(sk, na)
]

Alice -> Bob: [pk], na, sg

principal Bob[
	_ = SIGNVERIF(pk, na, sg)?
	h = HASH(na)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: na
]
